Undifferentiated Nausea
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Persistent functional feeling of queasiness | Complete absence of emesis or involuntary weight loss | Normal upper GI structural and metabolic workup

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-037
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of unremitting nausea requiring urgent evaluation to exclude an acute surgical abdomen or early bowel obstruction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate severe, sudden-onset nausea and abdominal discomfort. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out evolving appendicitis, biliary colic, or acute hollow viscous obstruction, preventing potentially fatal visceral compromise prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I constantly feel like I am going to throw up, my stomach feels incredibly full, but nothing actually comes out.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a persistent and recurrent feeling of sickness in the stomach that acutely worsened this evening. There is a complete absence of actual emesis or projectile vomiting. The patient describes vague postprandial fullness and discomfort. There is a lack of clear association with specific food intolerances. The patient maintains normal bowel habits without severe diarrhea. The patient specifically denies any associated severe headache, visual changes, or recent head trauma.
Functional Status: Resting uncomfortably on the couch.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; anxious about potential overnight deterioration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 136/88 mmHg | HR: 82 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears pale and mildly distressed by the nausea but is alert and communicative. Routine chart review notes a background of an asymptomatic presentation of borderline blood pressure (BP 130-139/85-89 mmHg) with a normal cardiovascular exam, completely separate from tonight's acute gastrointestinal complaint.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a soft, completely non-tender abdomen on deep palpation. There is no guarding, rigidity, or rebound tenderness (negative McBurney's and Murphy's signs). Bowel sounds are normal and active in all four quadrants. There is a complete absence of clinical signs of dehydration (moist mucous membranes, brisk capillary refill). Neurological exam is grossly intact.
Environmental Observations: Several half-eaten ginger candies and antacids scattered on the coffee table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No recent additions to medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Undifferentiated Nausea - Status: Acute functional dyspeptic flare.
Differentials:

Early Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out clinically due to the absence of bilious vomiting, lack of abdominal distension, and presence of normal, active bowel sounds.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out due to the complete lack of focal right lower quadrant tenderness and normal body temperature.

Central Nervous System Pathology (e.g., Increased ICP) - Ruled out due to the absence of severe headache, normal pupillary reflexes, and lack of projectile vomiting.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s severe, unremitting nausea raised immediate clinical concern for an early acute surgical abdomen, mandating an urgent after-hours assessment. Telephone triage accurately flagged the critical need to exclude an evolving bowel obstruction or acute biliary process. Meticulous abdominal palpation confirmed an entirely soft, non-peritonitic abdomen with completely normal bowel function, effectively ruling out acute surgical or obstructive pathologies. This prompt, definitive physical examination immediately provided safety and reassurance, avoiding a highly invasive and disruptive emergency department workup for a benign functional gastrointestinal disturbance.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral antiemetics. The expected investigation results include a normal comprehensive metabolic panel including liver enzymes and normal basic abdominal imaging, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via outpatient testing.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce the feeling of gastric fullness, consume only small, dry meals (like toast or crackers) every three hours and avoid drinking large volumes of water at the same time as food; separating liquids and solids physically reduces the stretch on your stomach wall, lowering the nausea reflex.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to assess response to antiemetics and arrange baseline liver function tests.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient begins vomiting blood or coffee-ground material, develops severe sharp abdominal pain, or stops passing gas or stool entirely.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; chronic or undifferentiated nausea requires formal metabolic and sonographic outpatient workup.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves episodic, non-life-threatening nausea heavily tied to gastric motility or dietary habits, improving with targeted symptomatic treatment.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if your stomach becomes hard to the touch, or if you develop a high fever alongside severe abdominal pain.

PATIENT EDUCATION

